@divider num="iii–v" kind="MODULES" title="Genres/and the Two Testaments" sub="What each kind of writing can and can't establish, and the shape of each half of the collection" foot="FOURTEEN PAGES"

@page id="m-genre" rh="LEARNING THE BIBLE · THE GENRE MAP · 1 OF 4" m="INFORMATION; I.3"
# Eight genres
lede: Genre determines what a passage is capable of establishing, and misreading it is the most common way a sincere reader reaches a confident wrong answer.
tabrl:
| Genre | What it's trying to do | What it can't establish |
| Law | Set a community's obligations, ritual, civil and moral, usually without separating them | Timeless policy. Legal codes address one society's cases. |
| Narrative | Tell what happened, shaped for a purpose. It often reports behavior without endorsing it. | Approval. A story about polygamy isn't an argument for it. |
| Poetry | Make something felt, through image, parallel lines and intensity | Doctrine built on a metaphor read literally |
| Wisdom | Teach how the world generally works, and in Job and Ecclesiastes, how often it doesn't | Promises. A proverb is a pattern, not a guarantee. |
| Prophecy | Deliver a message from God to a particular people at a particular moment: indictment, warning, consolation, sometimes prediction. Worship, idolatry and the character of God run throughout. | A calendar. Most prophecy speaks to its own audience's present, though some looks ahead and later readers read it forward. |
| Gospel | Present a life so the hearer has to reckon with it. Ancient biography with a purpose. | A transcript. Selection and arrangement are part of the form. |
| Letter | Address a live situation in a named community, often with sustained theological argument | Rules without context. You're reading one side of a conversation. |
| Apocalyptic | Reveal what's really happening behind visible power, in symbols, for people who can't say it plainly | Forecasts of technology. Its symbols were readable to its first audience. |
box: What genre error costs
Flattening a metaphor into a rule, or a story into an endorsement, has done real harm and still does. It's one mechanism among several, beside deliberate misuse, selective quotation and institutional interest, and it's worth naming exactly instead of blaming it for everything.
/end
prompt: The genre I was taught to read wrong | fill

@page rh="THE GENRES · SMALLER FORMS AND THE TEST · 2 OF 4" m="MATT 1; PS 88; LUKE 15; ISA 13–23"
# Forms inside the forms
lede: Eight genres is the map. These are the units a passage is actually built from, and naming the unit usually settles what it can be used for.
rec:
Genealogy | A claim about belonging and legitimacy. Who's included is the argument. Matthew's four women are the point of Matthew's list.
Lament | Structured complaint, often addressed to God and often unresolved. Its place in the canon makes protest a sanctioned form of prayer.
Hymn | Communal praise, often older than the book it sits in, and often quoted because it was already familiar.
Proverb | A compressed observation. Reliable as a pattern, dangerous as a promise, and cruel as a rebuke to someone suffering.
Parable | A story built to move the hearer. It usually turns on one hinge, and allegorizing every detail is how it gets defused.
Oracle | A delivered message with a named addressee, often a foreign nation. Reading an oracle addressed to Babylon as addressed to you is a category error with a long history.
Legal code | Case law with penalties, comparable to other ancient Near Eastern codes. Comparison is where the distinctive parts show.
Court history | A record written near power. Ask who commissioned it and what it leaves out.
box: The genre test, before quoting anything
1. What kind of writing is this?
2. What is this kind of writing for?
3. What can it establish?
4. What can't it establish?
5. Am I using it for the thing it can do?
6. If not, is the misuse mine, or my character's?
/end
prompt: The genre the Empire misreads on purpose, and the one it misreads sincerely | fill

