@page id="m-ot" rh="LEARNING THE BIBLE · SHAPE OF THE OLD TESTAMENT · 1 OF 5" m="INFORMATION; GEN–MAL"
# Six movements
lede: An orientation, not a summary. The collection isn't arranged as one story, and these movements overlap, double back, and in places disagree about what happened.
tabrl:
| Movement | What it's about | The question it leaves open |
| Beginnings · Genesis 1–11 | Creation, rupture, flood and the scattering of peoples. Two creation accounts sit side by side, never reconciled. | Whether human power over others is part of the design or part of the damage |
| The families · Genesis 12–50 | A promise attached to one household and carried through four generations of deception, infertility, favoritism and migration | Why the promise keeps passing through the person who shouldn't have received it |
| Exodus and law · Exodus–Deuteronomy | Liberation from imperial forced labor, forty years of learning to be a people, and a law given before the land is entered | Whether a people freed from empire can make law without reproducing it |
| Land and kings · Joshua–Kings | Settlement, tribal instability, the demand for a king, David's house, two kingdoms and the slow loss of both | Whether monarchy was a concession, a failure or the plan |
| Exile and return · Ezra, Nehemiah, Chronicles | Deportation, survival under foreign rule, partial return, rebuilding and a fierce argument about who belongs | Who counts, who's dissolved out of the community, and who writes the record |
| Wisdom and prophecy · across the collection | Two bodies of writing that comment on all the rest, one asking how to live well, one indicting how power behaves | Whether either one ever changed anything |
box: This companion's lens, declared
INTERPRETATION: Land runs through most of these movements: possession, loss, return, and who may hold it. It's a real and recurring concern of the collection. Reading the whole Old Testament as fundamentally about land is this companion's working lens, chosen because the Empire is a land empire. It isn't a neutral description of the material.
/end
prompt: Which movement my novel is secretly retelling | fill

@page rh="SHAPE OF THE OLD TESTAMENT · TWO ORDERINGS · 2 OF 5" m="2 CHR 36.23; MAL 4.5–6; 2 KGS 17; 2 KGS 25"
# Where the story stops
lede: The same books in two sequences, with two different endings, and so two different claims about what the collection is doing.
cols:
k: Tanakh order
Torah, then Prophets, then Writings, closing with Chronicles and Cyrus's decree to go up and rebuild. It ends on return. INTERPRETATION: The arc is instruction, then the record of failing it, then the literature of living with that, and the last word is permission to go home.
||
k: Christian Old Testament order
Law, history, poetry, prophets, closing with Malachi and a promise of Elijah's return. It ends on anticipation. INTERPRETATION: Resequenced this way, the collection reads as unfinished, which makes a New Testament feel like a continuation.
/end
k: A rough historical spine
tabrl:
| When | What happened |
| About 1000 BCE | The united monarchy of the biblical narrative, David and Solomon. CONTESTED: Its scale is actively debated by archaeologists. |
| About 930 BCE | Division into Israel in the north and Judah in the south. Two kingdoms, two records, one collection. |
| 722 BCE | Assyria destroys the northern kingdom. Deportation and dispersal. |
| 587/586 BCE | Babylon destroys Jerusalem and the Temple and deports the elite. Much of the collection is written, edited or compiled in the shadow of this. |
| 539 BCE | Persia takes Babylon, and return is permitted. Restoration is partial, contested and run under imperial authority. |
| 332–63 BCE | Greek, then Roman, rule. Apocalyptic writing develops under occupation. |
prompt: Which ordering the Empire prints, and what ending it needs its citizens to expect | fill

@page rh="SHAPE OF THE OLD TESTAMENT · FOUR STRONG CLAIMS · 3 OF 5" m="JUDG 21.25; 1 SAM 8; RUTH 4; 1–2 CHR"
# Say it more carefully
lede: Four claims worth keeping, restated so they survive contact with someone who knows the material. The memorable version is on the left and the defensible version on the right, and the difference between them is the discipline of this companion.
tab:
| The memorable version | What's actually defensible |
| Judges says there was no king, so the book wants one. | The refrain, everyone doing what was right in his own eyes because there was no king, sits in a collection that's divided about monarchy. Samuel records the demand for a king as a rejection of God and lists what a king will take. Judges names the cost of no central authority without settling the argument. |
| Ruth answers it quietly. | INTERPRETATION: A good reading. A Moabite woman's loyalty placed in David's genealogy reads as a counterargument to exclusion. The book doesn't announce itself as a rebuttal, and it can't be dated closely enough to prove intent. |
| Chronicles is the first act of imperial editing in the book itself. | Too confident. Chronicles retells Samuel and Kings with visible selection, David's worst material largely absent and worship in the foreground. It's an excellent case of a community re-telling its past for present purposes. Calling it the first, or imperial, overstates what can be shown. |
| The prophets are about how power treats the poor. | They're substantially about that, and reducing them to it loses most of the material: idolatry and false worship, foreign alliances, neighboring nations, ritual, exile and consolation, and the character of God. Keep the social indictment central without letting it become the whole subject. |
box: Why this page exists
A character who makes the left-hand claim can be brilliant and still wrong, and that's useful. A companion that makes it is unreliable, and in two years you won't be able to tell which of your own pages to trust.
/end
prompt: Which character makes the left-column argument, and who corrects her | fill

