@page id="m-nt" rh="LEARNING THE BIBLE · SHAPE OF THE NEW TESTAMENT · 1 OF 5" m="INFORMATION; ROMAN JUDEA; ACTS 2.44–45"
# Twenty-seven documents
lede: Written across roughly a century by a movement inside the Roman Empire that had no state power. It wasn't powerless in every sense, and its social position varied by city, decade and class.
k: The five kinds of document
rec:
Gospels · 4 | Narrative accounts of Jesus, written decades after the events, each for a particular community with particular concerns
Acts · 1 | The movement's spread from Jerusalem outward, by the author of Luke, as the second volume of one work
Pauline letters · 13 | Letters to particular congregations about particular problems. Several contain sustained theological argument, and Romans is a worked case.
General letters · 8 | Hebrews and the letters attributed to James, Peter, John and Jude, for wider audiences and varied purposes
Apocalypse · 1 | Revelation, a genre with its own conventions
k: Composition order isn't canonical order
Paul's letters come first in time, written in the 50s before any gospel existed. The collection opens with the gospels because it's arranged as a story and not as an archive. Read in the order written, it shows a movement working out what it believed before anyone had written down the life it believed about.
k: The Roman setting, without flattening it
Occupation, client kings, taxation, crucifixion as public policy, the imperial cult, and a legal status for the movement that changed by place and period. It was tolerated in some cities, prosecuted in others, and not systematically outlawed across the empire for most of this century. Acts shows shared property in Jerusalem specifically, and Paul's letters show congregations with property, patrons and households.
box: For TKC
INTERPRETATION: The useful fact isn't that the early movement was poor or illegal. A religion born under an empire was later administered by one, and the same texts served both.
/end

@page rh="SHAPE OF THE NEW TESTAMENT · THE FOUR ACCOUNTS · 2 OF 5" m="MARK; MATT; LUKE; JOHN; PART III, THE GOSPEL GRID"
# Four accounts, one subject
lede: They differ in selection, arrangement, emphasis and detail, and the differences are the most informative thing about them, because each shows what a particular community needed the story to do.
tabrl:
| Gospel | About | What it emphasizes | Where it begins |
| Mark | 65–75 | Urgency, secrecy, suffering. Shortest, probably earliest, and the source most scholars think Matthew and Luke used. | An adult at the river. No birth story. |
| Matthew | 80–90 | Fulfillment of Israel's scriptures, teaching in five great blocks, a new Moses. Written for a community deeply engaged with Jewish law. | A genealogy with four women in it, one of them Ruth |
| Luke | 80–95 | The poor, women, outsiders and reversal, the powerful pulled down and the hungry filled. Volume one of two. | Two pregnancies and a song about thrones being emptied |
| John | 90–110 | Identity and glory. Long discourses, no parables of the familiar kind, a different chronology and mostly its own material. | Before creation |
k: Why four matters for a novelist
The canon keeps four versions instead of harmonizing them into one, and that decision shows on every page. INTERPRETATION: An empire that wanted one official account had four to choose from and couldn't quietly reduce them, so the next best thing was deciding which one is read at which festival.
contested: Acts, and what it's doing
A narrative of expansion from Jerusalem to Rome, shaped to show the movement's legitimacy and reach. Where Acts and Paul's own letters describe the same events they don't always agree, and historians treat the gap as real.
/end
prompt: Which gospel the Empire reads aloud most, and which one it would rather not | fill

@page rh="SHAPE OF THE NEW TESTAMENT · THE LETTERS · 3 OF 5" m="ROM 16; 1 COR 14.34–35; EPH 5; COL 3; 1 TIM 2.11–15"
# Who wrote what
lede: Authorship is a scholarly question, not a settled fact, and the distinctions matter for TKC, because the letters most often quoted about households and women's silence are among those whose authorship is most disputed.
tabrl:
| Group | Scholarly view | Why it matters here |
| Undisputed Paul · Rom, 1–2 Cor, Gal, Phil, 1 Thess, Phlm | Broad agreement | Here Paul names women as coworkers, a deacon and an apostle. CONTESTED: 1 Corinthians 14:34–35, the silence passage, sits in an undisputed letter, and some manuscripts place it after 14:40, so many scholars think it was added later. |
| Disputed Paul · Eph, Col, 2 Thess | Genuinely divided | Ephesians and Colossians carry the household codes. Whether Paul wrote them changes what "the same author" can be claimed to mean. |
| Pastorals · 1–2 Tim, Titus | Most critical scholars: later | The strictest instructions about women teaching are here. Say "the Pauline letters" and not "Paul" when arguing across this line. |
| Hebrews | Anonymous | By near consensus, it isn't Paul's. A sustained argument about priesthood and sacrifice, directly useful to any state that runs a temple. |
| Other attributed letters · Jas, 1–2 Pet, 1–3 Jn, Jude | Varies. 2 Peter most doubted. | Attributing a letter to a famous name was a convention with a range of practices, so flatly asserting or denying authorship is equally unsafe. |
box: The sentence to keep
INTERPRETATION: Several of the passages that restrict women most come from letters whose authorship, or whose placement in the text, is most in question. POSSIBLE: The Empire would never mention it, because the instruction's authority rests on the name attached.
/end
prompt: Which letters the Empire built its household law on, and who in the Empire knows the authorship problem | fill

@page rh="SHAPE OF THE NEW TESTAMENT · REVELATION · 4 OF 5" m="REV 13; REV 17–18; REV 4–5; EASTERN LECTIONARY"
# Four ways to read it
lede: All four are held by serious readers. This companion leans toward the first, and it's still a framework and not a fact about the text. In TKC the reading a character holds is a political position.
contested: I · Resistance literature against Rome
Written for congregations under imperial pressure, using the conventions of apocalyptic to say what couldn't be said plainly. Strong internal support: seven cities, the imperial cult, a city on seven hills, a luxury economy described with contempt. Most historical-critical scholarship works here.
/end
contested: II · A sequence of future events
Read as a program of things still to come. Widely held in popular and some confessional traditions, with enormous cultural influence and a long record of specific predictions failing, which is part of the material too.
/end
contested: III · A cycle, not a timeline
The visions as recurring patterns of empire, judgment and endurance. Explains the repetition, seals, trumpets and bowls covering the same ground three times, better than a sequential reading does.
/end
contested: IV · Liturgy and vision
The book as worship: hymns, thrones, incense and a heavenly assembly, meant to reorder what the hearer thinks is ultimately in charge. The Eastern churches' reluctance to read it aloud sits oddly beside this, and both facts are true.
/end
box: The two identifications everyone makes
INTERPRETATION: The beast as an imperial power and the woman on the beast as a capital city are the readings the evidence best supports, and they're readings. What's nearly beyond argument is narrower: the book's targets are political and economic, and it expects its audience to recognize them.
/end
prompt: Which reading is official in the Empire, and which one gets someone arrested | fill

